// The fast Fourier transform for sysl, generic over the float width.
//
// Cooley-Tukey radix 2, in place, beside the O(n^2) sum that is the transform's definition -- and
// the second is shipped rather than kept in the tests, because a rearrangement is exactly the kind
// of thing that can be subtly wrong and still produce plausible numbers.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       fft { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/fft", version = "0.1.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `fft`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.fft`. It
// declares no dependencies of its own -- everything it needs is the standard library's `sysl.math`,
// `sysl.math.complex` and `sysl.slices`.

package {
  name    = "fft"
  version = "0.1.0"

  // Nothing here reaches past what 0.0.79 shipped. The generic body needs three things and all three
  // are older than that: `Complex[F]` over a `Float` bound, `F(n)` casting an index to the type
  // parameter, and `sysl.slices.sort_stable_by` for the peak ordering.
  sysl = "0.0.79"
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is worth knowing which half asks for it.** `fft` and `ifft` transform the
// samples where they lie and allocate nothing at all -- that is deliberate, and it is what a program
// on a board would use. Everything that builds a *new* signal needs storage whose size is only known
// when it is called: `dft`, `real_signal`, `copy_of`, `fft_of`, `ifft_of`, `magnitudes`, both
// convolutions, and `peaks`.
//
// A capability clause is a property of the package rather than of a function, so declaring it here
// is the honest answer even though the core transform would satisfy `no alloc` on its own. A caller
// that wants only the in-place pair still pays this declaration; splitting the package to avoid that
// would be splitting it along a line no caller reads it along.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything.
requires {
  heap = true
}
